          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "けいやくする — keiyaku suru — \"to sign a contract\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "じょうけんに さんせいしたら、けいやくします。 — jōken ni sansei shitara, keiyaku shimasu — \"If I agree to the terms, I will sign the contract.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Polite けいやくします, keiyakushimasu; a する verb. Past (polite): ちちが かいしゃと けいやくしました。, chichi ga kaisha to keiyaku shimashita."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "けいやくする (keiyaku suru) is built from a Sino-Japanese noun and the native verb suru, \"to do\". The noun, keiyaku, is two Chinese characters read with their on'yomi, readings borrowed from Chinese: kei, \"pledge\" (Middle Chinese khejH) and yaku, \"promise\" ('jak) (KANJIDIC2; JMdict; Middle Chinese in Baxter's transcription)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying what happens if."
            }
